ch higher than I would have  thought it's very fast growing education  that the premium families  that those who can often put their  children and struggle to keep them there  we're talking about 11 to 16 year olds  so in the context of boys having to  really work for the castle economy and  girls at the age of marriage it's quite  remarkable that those children still get  to school stop because of curtailed  education and Taliban some 16 eurozone  primary classes of course so it's all a  mixed mix of Ages and is it equal boys  and girls or are there more boys than  girls there's obviously more boys and  girls about three-quarters of boys and  the lesser proportion of of girls but  they have girl in boys schools very  often then the the other point to make  was really that when you're walking in  working in conflict settings you're very  often guided by a paradigm that mental  health is consequent of trauma of war  and I wanted to broaden that agenda a  little bit to think about risking them  in a more interdisciplinary way so I was  trying to marry the best of Psychiatry  of the best of Social Sciences and in  psychiatry you want to understand what  mental this health disorders are and who  has them so you can treat individuals in  the social sciences you might want to  think about how this suffering comes  about and why it's occurring and how  it's experienced and coped with so how  its articulated in a society setting and  was it difficult to do the work in the  schools how were you received as I say  we worked through local partners so we  had a Ministry of Education letter which  helps open doors we had we worked  through Social Work professor in  Peshawar and psychiatrist in Kabul I'm  an anthropologist my research partner  mark Eckerman is a Middle Eastern  specialist so we've put together the  kind of team that would be able to be  very effective we implemented it with a  Kabul based research agency called Altai  and it was funded by medical charity  it was 